Installing the Required Packages
================================

AlphaDict depends:
  1) Qt5 >= 5.9: "https://www.qt.io/"
     The GUI needs Qt5Core/Qt5Gui/Qt5Widgets; the newer QML interface also needs
     Qt5Qml/Qt5Quick/Qt5QuickWidgets. When those modules are missing the build
     falls back to the QtWidgets interface.
  2) boost (system, filesystem): "https://www.boost.org/"
  3) sqlite3: "https://www.sqlite.org" -- optional. Without a system sqlite3 the
     bundled amalgamation in src/lib/sqlite3 is used, so no -dev package is needed.
  4) pkg-config: configure locates Qt and sqlite3 through it instead of
     hard-coding include and library paths, so multiarch layouts (Debian/Ubuntu
     x86_64-linux-gnu) and custom prefixes work unchanged.
  5) X11 (libX11): needed by the screen word capture. libqt5x11extras5 is no
     longer required.

Debian / Ubuntu:
    sudo apt-get install build-essential pkg-config \
        qtbase5-dev qtdeclarative5-dev \
        libboost-dev libboost-filesystem-dev libx11-dev
    # optional, to use the system sqlite3 instead of the bundled one:
    #   sudo apt-get install libsqlite3-dev
    # to regenerate the translations (make uitr):
    #   sudo apt-get install qttools5-dev-tools

Fedora / RHEL:
    sudo dnf install gcc-c++ make pkgconf-pkg-config \
        qt5-qtbase-devel qt5-qtdeclarative-devel \
        boost-devel libX11-devel

Note: this code base is Qt5 only. When a Qt6 development package is the only one
installed, configure stops with an explicit error, because the QtWidgets code in
src/gui/qt still uses classes that Qt6 moved or removed (QShortcut/QAction to
QtGui, QRegExp/QTextCodec to Core5Compat, QDesktopWidget is gone); that port is
not done yet.


How to Compile
==============

linux,unix
----------
configure and the Makefile live in src/:

$ cd src
$ ./configure  
$ ./configure --help
  can give you some useful information, if you get trouble and want to set some 
  custom configurations to fix it.

$ make 
  builds both binaries: AlphaDict (GUI) and AldConvert (dictionary converter),
  and uses AldConvert to turn system/dicts/en-ch.xml into the sample dictionary
  en-ch.aldict. A fresh clone has no dictionary at all, so this gives the GUI
  60 words to show on first start. Rebuild just the dictionary with: make dict

$ make install
  --prefix defaults to /usr/local; the install rules do not sudo for you.
  The sample dictionary is installed too; replace it with your own .aldict.

$ AlphaDict
  Now, You can enjoy 'AlphaDict'. 

$ make uninstall

$ make uitr
  make muti-language.  Needs lupdate/lrelease (Debian: qttools5-dev-tools); the
  path comes from the Qt bin directory reported by pkg-config, and can be
  overridden with --with-qttool.

Useful configure options:
  --prefix=DIR        installation prefix                   [default /usr/local]
  --enable-qml        build the QML interface               [autodetect]
  --disable-qml       build the legacy QtWidgets interface only
  --disable-gui       skip the GUI, build the command line tools only
                      (alias --disable-qt5; --enable-gui is an alias of --enable-qt5)
  --enable-debug      debug build                           [off]
  --enable-qt6        rejected: this code base is Qt5 only (see above)
  --with-qttool=PATH  directory holding moc/uic/rcc         [autodetect]
  --extra-cflags/--extra-ldflags/--extra-libs   extra compiler/linker flags

Qt is detected with pkg-config only: Qt5Core, Qt5Gui and Qt5Widgets must all be
present, and the version must be at least 5.9. When Qt is missing or too old the
script stops and says why instead of falling back to a guessed path. With the QML
interface enabled, config.mak holds GUI=yes QML=yes, i.e. CONFIG_QT5 and
CONFIG_QML in config.h.

windows
-------
The Visual Studio project files are no longer shipped: they were frozen at
Qt5.3/QtWidgets, referenced generated headers that no longer exist and could
not build the current sources. The windows sources under src/ (src/win32 holds
the platform glue) are still there; build them with a Qt5 MSVC or MinGW kit and
your own project file, or use the maintained configure + make build on
linux/unix.


How to Make Dictionaries
========================

AlphaDict has a open dictionary format, see "doc/(dictionary_inner.pdf,front-end_format.xml)"
for more detail, encourage you to make your own dictionary.

Usually, you only need to know the 'front-end_format.xml',There are two steps to make a dictioanry.
1) Create a xx.xml accoring to front-end_format.xml format.
2) Use AldConvert(.exe) to convert xx.xml to xx.aldict.
   $ AldConvert -o xx xx.xml
   AldConvert shall create a dictionary: xx.aldict.
   
AldConvert supports multi-xml files. You can split a large xml into a few files,
or merge a few xml files into a dict.
  $ AldConvert -o xx xx.xml  xx_2.xml  xx_3.xml 

doc/alphadict_en-ch.xml is a example, You can refrence them to build your dictionary.


Where to Put Dictionaries
=========================

For windows    :  'installing direcotry'/system/dicts
For linux, unix:  /usr/local/share/alphadict/dicts


How to Use the Third-Party Dictionaries
=======================================

AlphaDict don't support some dict formats directly but provides some tools to convert 
these third-party dictionaries to AlphaDict's front-end xml. 

It's a little annoy, does it? Anyway,These tools are all python scripts, pretty simple,
They are easy to use or modify, they are at 
    /usr/local/share/alphadict/tools  -- linux, unix
    'installing direcotry'/tools -- win32.
I will make some dictionaries previously, but the best way is to convert by yourself, 
because these dictionaries will update from time to time.

$ python xx_toaldictxml.py
  will show usage.

stardict
--------
1) put all the dictionary files to a directory, like foo.
   foo/ [foo_xx.idx, foo_xx.ifo, foo_xx.dict]
2) $ python stardict_to_aldictxml.py -i foo/foo_xx  -o  foo_xx.xml
3) $ AldConvert -o foo_xx   foo_xx.xml
     you will get foo_xx.aldict

You can downlaod the dictionaries of stardict from here:
    "http://abloz.com/huzheng/stardict-dic"

cedict
------
Currently, only support 'text' format, please download 'text' insteadof 'xml' format.

$ python cedict_to_aldictxml.py -i foo.txt
$ AldConvert -o foo  foo.xml
    you will get foo.aldict

You can download the cedict dicionary from here:
    "http://www.mdbg.net/chindict/chindict.php?page=cedict"

dicts.info
----------
You can download the bilingual dictionaries from here:
    "http://www.dicts.info/uddl.php"
Select "File format: UTF-8 plain text", then "Download dictionary".

Here is a example for coverting.

$ ./dictsinfo_to_aldictxml.py  -i english-chinese-xx.txt
$ AldConvert -o dictsinfo_english-chinese   english-chinese-xx.xml


IDP
---
You can download the dictionary files from here:
    "http://www.june29.com/IDP/IDPfiles.html"
Lookup "Dictionary Files"

Here is a example for coverting.

$ ./idp_to_aldictxml.py  -i French.txt
$ AldConvert -o idp_french   French.xml
 

